{
 "id": "sorites",
 "channel": "philosophy",
 "style": "clay",
 "title": "THE HEAP PARADOX",
 "angle": "Where exactly does a heap begin?",
 "answer": "Logic says either heaps don't exist or one grain makes the difference; Williamson's epistemicism bites the bullet (a sharp line exists but is unknowable), while engineers treat truth as a matter of degree (fuzzy logic ran Sendai's subway from 1987); in practice someone has to draw the line, and the real question is who.",
 "logline": "One grain is no heap and one more never makes one, so where does a heap begin? Each step looks safe, so either heaps don't exist or one grain decides, but it isn't about sand: baldness, adulthood, colour all blur, so Chrysippus advised falling silent, but Williamson argued a sharp line exists that we can never know, but engineers bet on blur and ran a subway on degrees of truth, so the real question is who draws the line.",
 "hook_text": "ONE GRAIN AT A TIME",
 "hooks": [
  "A philosopher argues one single grain of sand turns a non-heap into a heap.",
  "In 1987, a Japanese subway started braking with 'a little' and 'quite'."
 ],
 "world": {
  "scenery": "open",
  "ground": "#EBDDB4"
 },
 "script": [
  {
   "text": "One grain of sand isn't a heap. Neither are two.",
   "beat": "hook"
  },
  {
   "text": "Add one grain at a time, and by that logic, a million grains still aren't a heap.",
   "beat": "hook",
   "link": "so"
  },
  {
   "text": "So where exactly does a heap begin?",
   "beat": "question",
   "link": "so"
  },
  {
   "text": "Greek logicians asked this 2,400 years ago; sorites means heap.",
   "say": "Greek logicians asked this twenty-four hundred years ago; so-rye-teez means heap.",
   "beat": "reveal1",
   "link": "answer"
  },
  {
   "text": "The same thinker is credited with the liar paradox: this sentence is false.",
   "beat": "reveal1",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "Each step looks safe: one grain can't turn a non-heap into a heap.",
   "beat": "reveal1",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "So either there are no heaps at all, or one grain makes all the difference.",
   "beat": "reveal1",
   "link": "so"
  },
  {
   "text": "But this was never really about sand.",
   "beat": "reveal2",
   "link": "but"
  },
  {
   "text": "When does a man go bald, or does red turn orange in a rainbow?",
   "beat": "reveal2",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "The Stoic Chrysippus advised simply falling silent as you near the border.",
   "say": "The Stoic Kry-sip-us advised simply falling silent as you near the border.",
   "beat": "reveal2",
   "link": "so"
  },
  {
   "text": "But in 1994, Timothy Williamson defended the shocking option.",
   "say": "But in nineteen ninety-four, Timothy Williamson defended the shocking option.",
   "beat": "reveal3",
   "link": "but"
  },
  {
   "text": "There is a sharp line, he argued: one exact grain makes the heap.",
   "beat": "reveal3",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "We just can never know which grain it is.",
   "beat": "reveal3",
   "link": "but"
  },
  {
   "text": "It sounds absurd, but it's the only answer that keeps ordinary logic intact.",
   "beat": "reveal3",
   "link": "but"
  },
  {
   "text": "But engineers bet on blur instead.",
   "beat": "turn",
   "link": "but"
  },
  {
   "text": "In 1987, Sendai's subway began braking by fuzzy logic: a little, quite, very.",
   "say": "In nineteen eighty-seven, Sendai's subway began braking by fuzzy logic: a little, quite, very.",
   "beat": "turn",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "So maybe truth comes in degrees, and the real question isn't where the line is.",
   "beat": "payoff",
   "link": "so"
  },
  {
   "text": "Eighteen to vote, one grain for a heap: who gets to draw your lines?",
   "beat": "payoff",
   "link": "so"
  }
 ],
